    "seoTitle": "Citizenship-Threshold Property Searches",
    "metaDescription": "Selecting and preparing documents for properties that meet the real-estate investment threshold for citizenship; applying is no guarantee of approval.",
    "body": "## What is the Turkish citizenship pathway through real estate investment?\n\nTurkish citizenship through real estate investment is one of the \"exceptional naturalization\" categories set out in the implementing regulation of the Turkish Citizenship Law, alongside other categories such as industrial investment or providing an exceptional service to the state. This pathway is based on buying a property that meets a legally defined minimum value, with that value confirmed through an official real estate valuation report, and committing before the land registry directorate not to sell the property for a set period. This pathway is entirely different from the standard real estate residence permit, since the latter grants only residence with no effect on citizenship, and it is also different from Turkey's general citizenship services, which include other pathways unrelated to buying property. The final decision to grant citizenship through this pathway is not automatic; even when the investment criteria are met, the decision remains subject to the discretion of the competent authorities.\n\n## General requirements for this pathway\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n| Official real estate valuation report | Issued by an accredited valuation body, confirming that the property's value meets the legal minimum required specifically for this pathway |\n| Commitment not to sell for a set period | Recorded as an annotation on the title deed, and prohibits disposing of the property before the legally set period expires |\n| Title deed free of restrictions | The property must be free of legal disputes or restrictions that would prevent registering the no-sale condition |\n| Value matching across official channels | The property's value in the sale contract, the official valuation, and the land registry must match, with proof that the amount was transferred through official banking channels |\n| Property type and number of properties | The investment can be in one property or more, provided the total minimum value is met and each property has its own independent report |\n\n## Important points to know\n\nThe legal minimum value for this pathway has been amended more than once since this exception was introduced, so any figure circulating online or from unofficial sources may be outdated and unreliable.\n\nThe no-sale commitment is not a formality; it is a legal condition officially recorded in the land registry, and any attempt to circumvent it or dispose of the property before the period ends can expose the citizenship file to review or cancellation.\n\nMeeting the investment criteria — value, valuation, and the no-sale commitment — is a necessary but not sufficient condition on its own; final approval of the citizenship grant remains a discretionary decision in the hands of the competent official authorities, and there is no guarantee the application will succeed automatically.\n\nSimply owning property in Turkey, no matter its value, does not automatically entitle the owner to apply through this pathway unless the full formal and procedural requirements specifically tied to it are met, including registering the purpose of obtaining citizenship within the purchase transaction itself.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Does buying any property in Turkey qualify its owner for citizenship?**\nNo. The property must meet the legal minimum value set specifically for this pathway, confirmed by an official valuation report, with a no-sale commitment registered, and the transaction must be carried out in the way that qualifies the property as the basis for an exceptional citizenship application.\n\n**Does meeting the required property value guarantee citizenship?**\nNo. Meeting the value, valuation, and no-sale commitment requirements only makes the file eligible to apply; the final decision remains subject to the discretion of the competent official authorities, and the application can be rejected even when these formal requirements are met.\n\n**Can the property be sold immediately after obtaining citizenship?**\nNo. The no-sale commitment is officially recorded on the title deed for a legally set period, and disposing of the property before this period ends violates the condition on which the citizenship application was originally based, and can expose the granted citizenship to reconsideration.\n\n**Is this pathway the same as the standard real estate residence permit?**\nNo. The real estate residence permit grants only the right to reside in Turkey by buying a property of a much lower value, with no effect on citizenship. The citizenship-through-investment pathway, by contrast, is an independent route with much higher value requirements and entirely different procedures, and its legal effect is acquiring Turkish citizenship itself.",
