    "seoTitle": "Turkish SIM card registration help",
    "metaDescription": "Getting a Turkish SIM in your name, plus key guidance on the deadline to register a foreign phone before it's blocked from Turkish networks.",
    "body": "## What you should know about your mobile line and foreign phone in Turkey\n\nGetting a mobile line in Turkey is regulated under the Turkish Information and Communication Technologies Authority (BTK), and no line may be registered in anyone's name without valid official identification. Foreigners are generally required to present a passport, and proof of residence may also be requested depending on the type of subscription. This isn't a formality — it's part of a legal framework that ties every line to a verified real identity.\n\nA less well-known but critical point concerns the phone itself: under Law No. 5809 and the regulations issued by BTK, any foreign mobile device (purchased outside Turkey) used with a Turkish line for longer than a legally defined period must be registered in BTK's electronic device identification (IMEI) system, or the device will be blocked from operating on Turkish networks. The period set under current regulation is 120 days, starting from the first moment a Turkish SIM is inserted into the device. This is an actual legal obligation, not just advice — a device that isn't registered once this deadline passes stops working with any Turkish line until its status is resolved.\n\n## Key points to know\n\n- Many newcomers are unaware this deadline exists, and are caught off guard when their personal phone loses network access after months of normal use\n- The obligation applies to the device (its IMEI number), not just the line — switching SIM cards or carriers does not reset the deadline or cancel the obligation\n- It's important to know this before relying on a foreign phone as your main, long-term means of communication in Turkey, to avoid a sudden disruption to your communication, work, or official dealings that require a working number\n- Registering the line itself always requires a real, valid form of identification — no provider can activate a line without ID that meets the required standards\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Can I keep using my foreign phone indefinitely with a Turkish SIM?**\nNo. Under BTK regulation, a foreign device may be used with a Turkish line for a set period (120 days from the first Turkish SIM being inserted) before registering the device in BTK's electronic identification system becomes mandatory for it to keep working on Turkish networks.\n\n**What happens if my phone isn't registered within this deadline?**\nThe system blocks the device from all Turkish networks based on its IMEI number, regardless of which SIM or carrier is used, and the device won't work with any Turkish line again until its status is resolved through the official process.\n\n**Does it make a difference if I'm a resident rather than a tourist?**\nYes, in terms of the documents required — the rules take a resident's status (residence permit) into account differently from a visitor with only a passport — but the underlying obligation to register the device within the legal deadline applies in both cases.",
